\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Four of the five segmentation error types on one cohort tree (left coronary tree of scan 14, front view, line width proportional to radius). The fifth (T5) changes only the throat radius of the lesion in (a). (a) The tree with the host LAD in blue, an inserted 80\% DS proximal lesion and the measurement point 20~mm beyond it. (b) T1 deletes the largest side branch beyond the lesion (red). (c) T2 ends the host vessel 25~mm beyond the distal edge of the lesion; the red part is lost. (d) Radius along the LAD for the caliber errors, T3 lengthening the lesion by 2.46~mm and T4 shrinking the radius by 7\% from the lesion onwards. This instance was also solved in 3D without the lesion, with the lesion, and with T1.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:design}{{1}{3}{Four of the five segmentation error types on one cohort tree (left coronary tree of scan 14, front view, line width proportional to radius). The fifth (T5) changes only the throat radius of the lesion in (a). (a) The tree with the host LAD in blue, an inserted 80\% DS proximal lesion and the measurement point 20~mm beyond it. (b) T1 deletes the largest side branch beyond the lesion (red). (c) T2 ends the host vessel 25~mm beyond the distal edge of the lesion; the red part is lost. (d) Radius along the LAD for the caliber errors, T3 lengthening the lesion by 2.46~mm and T4 shrinking the radius by 7\% from the lesion onwards. This instance was also solved in 3D without the lesion, with the lesion, and with T1}{figure.1}{}}
